Day 66 - Valid Parentheses

Problem Statement:
Given a string s containing (, ), {, }, [ and ], determine whether the brackets are valid.

Code:
class Solution {
public:
    bool isValid(string s) {
        stack<char> st;

        for(int i = 0; i < s.size(); i++) {

            if(s[i] == '(' || s[i] == '[' || s[i] == '{') {
                st.push(s[i]);
            }
            else {
                if(st.empty()) {
                    return false;
                }

                if((s[i] == ')' && st.top() == '(') ||
                   (s[i] == ']' && st.top() == '[') ||
                   (s[i] == '}' && st.top() == '{')) {

                    st.pop();
                }
                else {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }

        return st.empty()
    }
};






Time Complexity:
O(n)

Space Complexity:
O(n)

Approach:
This solution uses a stack because brackets follow the LIFO (Last In, First Out) principle.

Push every opening bracket into the stack.
For every closing bracket, first check whether the stack is empty.
If the stack is not empty, compare the closing bracket with st.top().
If they match, pop the opening bracket.
If they don't match, return false.
At the end, the stack must be empty for the string to be valid.

